+# CHG-062 performance decision — proposal, not approved
+
+The shared About → @ Contact → Settings header is implemented locally. Application publication remains blocked by the original Chromium first-Settings-interaction timing gate. No exception has been applied.
+
+## Measured evidence
+
+Original Chromium run40201179821: firstAction median45.4ms versus52.3ms, p9548.8ms versus55ms,39/40slower paired samples under4×CPU throttling. Original baseline, sample counts, bootstrap confidence and all other acceptance rules were unchanged. Current local1096tests/157suites, original coverage ratchet and8layout/chrome/accessibility workflows pass. Both pinned screenshot/integration editions and both WebKit performance jobs passed on preceding complete candidate40198168109. A final fresh complete candidate is required; preceding passes are not current acceptance.
+
+Pinned phase diagnostics show faster native navigation dispatch but more overall layout time. Grid, masks, wordmark wrapping, frozen-header, original Settings markup, forced scrolling and footer-formatting experiments did not demonstrate a sufficient improvement. They were diagnostic injections only and were not applied to the app. The exact cause remains unresolved; these results do not establish that the slowdown is unavoidable.
+
+## Narrow proposed exception
+
+Only if the owner explicitly approves: permit Chromium's first Settings interaction, in public and private-preview editions, to add at most10ms to both the paired baseline median and p95 under the existing4×CPU test. Continue reporting the historical difference and confidence evidence. Keep the immutable baseline, sample counts and runner controls. No allowance for WebKit or other metrics; preserve cold/warm startup, subsequent navigation/input, capture feedback, cleanup/memory and original raw/gzip payload limits. Deliberate slowdowns must still be rejected.
+
+This is an explicit change to the original no-regression requirement, not a bug fix or an already-approved policy. The alternative is retaining the original requirement and continuing targeted optimization. Approval of a budget change would not itself approve publication: regenerate matching media/docs and require complete acceptance of the exact candidate before publishing b. Main/dev remain frozen.
+
+Automatic release-cache refresh remains a separate planned task; no timestamps or broad storage clearing have been introduced.
